-- articles
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('kenalan-dengan-raga', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'memahami-raga' and locale = 'id'), 'Kenalan dengan Raga: Tur 5 Menit', 'Panduan singkat memakai Raga: dari mencatat berat pertama sampai membaca tren dan menjaga streak.', $md$## Raga itu ngapain sih?

Raga adalah kalkulator BMI yang dilengkapi riwayat, grafik tren, dan pengingat menimbang. Idenya sederhana: kamu cukup mencatat berat badan secara rutin, dan Raga yang menghitung, menyimpan, lalu menyajikan datanya dalam bentuk yang gampang dibaca. Tidak ada drama, tidak ada penilaian.

## Tab Hitung: titik awalmu

Di sinilah semuanya dimulai. Masukkan tinggi dan berat badanmu, tekan tombol Hitung BMI, dan kamu langsung dapat hasilnya: angka BMI, gauge berwarna, dan kategorinya. Kalau hasilnya mau disimpan, tekan Simpan ke riwayat. Kamu juga bisa bikin beberapa profil, misalnya buat anggota keluarga, dan ganti-ganti profil aktif lewat chip di atas.

## Tab Riwayat: ceritanya ada di sini

Satu angka timbangan tidak menceritakan apa-apa, tapi rangkaian angka selama berminggu-minggu bercerita banyak. Di tab Riwayat kamu bisa lihat grafik berat badan, ganti rentang waktu (7 hari sampai semua), dan baca insight otomatis seperti rata-rata, tren naik atau turun, dan perkiraan laju per minggu.

## Tab Kesehatan: angka di balik layar

Tab ini menghitung tiga hal dari datamu: BMR (energi yang dibakar tubuh saat istirahat total), kebutuhan kalori harian berdasarkan level aktivitas, dan rentang berat ideal untuk tinggimu. Penting: semuanya adalah ESTIMASI dari rumus umum, bukan vonis medis. Anggap sebagai titik awal diskusi, bukan kesimpulan akhir.

## Tab Streak, Arena, dan Profil

Streak mencatat konsistensi menimbang mingguanmu dan target berat yang kamu pasang. Arena adalah papan komunitas buat yang sudah login dan opt-in (default-nya mati, datamu tetap privat). Di Profil ada pengaturan unit, tema, pengingat, sampai backup cloud.

## Tips biar datanya jujur

Timbang di kondisi yang sama setiap kali: pagi hari, setelah ke kamar mandi, sebelum makan, dengan pakaian yang mirip. Catat secara rutin, dan jangan baper sama satu angka yang aneh — lihat trennya.

## Data kamu milikmu

Raga tidak punya iklan dan tidak menjual data. Semua catatanmu tersimpan dulu di HP-mu sendiri, dan aplikasi ini jalan penuh meski offline. Kalau kamu mau, ada backup cloud opsional supaya datamu aman kalau ganti HP, lengkap dengan kode backup yang cuma kamu yang pegang.

## Mulai dari mana?

Kalau baru pertama buka: selesaikan onboarding (nama, tinggi, berat awal), timbang sekali buat dapat angka pertama, dan jangan buru-buru pasang target. Pakai seminggu dulu buat kenal ritmenya. Target berat bisa dipasang nanti di tab Streak kalau kamu sudah siap, dan bisa diubah kapan saja.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'id') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('cara-baca-gauge-bmi', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'memahami-raga' and locale = 'id'), 'Cara Membaca Gauge BMI Tanpa Panik', 'Arti warna dan angka di gauge BMI Raga — plus satu hal penting: BMI itu alat skrining, bukan diagnosis.', $md$## Angkanya dari mana?

Gauge di Raga menampilkan Indeks Massa Tubuh (BMI) kamu, yaitu berat badan dalam kilogram dibagi kuadrat tinggi badan dalam meter. Misalnya berat 70 kg dan tinggi 1,70 m, maka BMI-nya 70 dibagi (1,70 × 1,70), sekitar 24,2. Raga menghitung ini otomatis setiap kamu mencatat.

## Warna dan kategorinya

Gauge memakai klasifikasi WHO untuk orang dewasa:

- Di bawah 18,5: berat badan kurang
- 18,5–24,9: normal
- 25–29,9: kelebihan berat badan
- 30 ke atas: obesitas

Setiap rentang punya warnanya sendiri supaya sekilas langsung kebaca. Kategori ini adalah patokan skrining populasi, bukan label yang menempel di jidatmu.

## Yang gauge TIDAK bilang

Ini bagian paling penting. Gauge tidak tahu komposisi tubuhmu — ia tidak bisa membedakan otot dan lemak. Ia juga tidak tahu usiamu, riwayat kesehatanmu, atau lemak di sekitar perutmu. Dua orang dengan angka gauge yang sama bisa punya kondisi yang sangat berbeda.

## Cara pakai gauge yang sehat

Pakai gauge sebagai kompas, bukan hakim. Kalau angkanya di luar rentang normal dan bertahan di sana berminggu-minggu, itu sinyal untuk cari tahu lebih lanjut — misalnya baca artikel keterbatasan BMI di Akademi BMI, atau diskusi dengan tenaga kesehatan profesional. Satu angka aneh sesekali? Abaikan, lihat trennya.

## Contoh cara mikirnya

Misalnya gauge menunjukkan 27, kategori kelebihan berat badan. Respon yang sehat bukan panik atau langsung diet ekstrem, melainkan: pertama, cek apakah angka ini konsisten selama beberapa minggu atau cuma anomali sesekali. Kedua, lihat konteks lain — lingkar pinggang, seberapa aktif kamu, riwayat kesehatan. Ketiga, kalau angkanya bertahan, jadikan bahan diskusi dengan tenaga profesional.

## Kapan angkanya perlu ditanggapi serius

Satu kategori di atas normal bukan vonis, tapi kalau BMI-mu 30 ke atas dan bertahan di sana, atau naik terus selama berbulan-bulan, itu layak ditanggapi serius. Bukan dengan menyalahkan diri sendiri, melainkan dengan mencari bantuan yang tepat: dokter, ahli gizi, atau sekadar mulai dari kebiasaan kecil yang dibahas di kategori Kebiasaan Sehat.$md$, 2, 'published', true, now(), now(), 'id') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('membaca-grafik-tren', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'memahami-raga' and locale = 'id'), 'Membaca Grafik Tren: Satu Angka Tidak Menceritakan Apa-apa', 'Berat badan naik-turun harian itu normal. Yang penting arah trennya, bukan angka hari ini.', $md$## Kenapa angka harian menipu

Berat badanmu bisa naik-turun 1–2 kg dalam sehari, dan itu wajar. Penyebabnya hal-hal sepele: banyak minum, makanan asin kemarin malam, isi pencernaan, atau buat sebagian orang, siklus hormonal. Tidak satu pun dari itu berarti kamu tiba-tiba jadi lebih gemuk atau lebih kurus dalam 24 jam.

## Aturan main menimbang

Supaya datanya bisa dibandingkan, bikin kondisinya konsisten: timbang di pagi hari, setelah ke kamar mandi, sebelum makan dan minum, dengan pakaian yang mirip. Frekuensinya tidak harus tiap hari — seminggu sekali di hari dan jam yang sama sudah cukup bagus buat kebanyakan orang.

## Cara baca grafik di Raga

Di tab Riwayat, ganti rentang waktunya dan perhatikan arah garisnya, bukan titik-titiknya. Garis yang pelan-pelan turun selama berminggu-minggu artinya usahamu jalan, walau ada titik yang naik di tengah jalan. Raga juga menampilkan rata-rata dan laju per minggu supaya kamu tidak perlu menebak-nebak.

## Kapan perlu waspada

Waspadai perubahan yang drastis dan tidak disengaja — misalnya berat turun atau naik banyak dalam waktu singkat tanpa perubahan pola makan atau aktivitas. Itu bukan soal diet, itu sinyal untuk konsultasi ke tenaga kesehatan profesional. Tren yang sehat itu pelan dan stabil, bukan dramatis.

## Rata-rata mingguan: trik sederhananya

Kalau angka harian bikin kamu overthinking, coba trik ini: timbang tiga kali seminggu di kondisi yang sama, lalu lihat rata-ratanya. Rata-rata mingguan jauh lebih stabil dan jujur dibanding angka harian mana pun. Bandingkan rata-rata minggu ini dengan minggu lalu — di situlah tren yang sebenarnya.

## Jangan kejar angka harian

Kesalahan paling umum: melihat timbangan naik hampir sekilo pagi ini lalu menghukum diri dengan makan super sedikit seharian. Itu seperti menyetir sambil menatap spion — reaksinya selalu terlambat dan salah arah. Keputusan soal pola makan harus dibuat dari tren mingguan, bukan dari satu angka pagi ini.

## Simpan konteksnya juga

Angka tanpa konteks gampang disalahartikan. Kalau kamu sakit, kurang tidur, atau sedang banyak acara makan, catat itu — Raga menyimpan riwayatmu apa adanya, dan konteks membantumu membaca ulang grafik berbulan-bulan kemudian tanpa menyalahkan diri sendiri atas minggu yang berantakan.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'id') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('streak-dan-konsistensi', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'memahami-raga' and locale = 'id'), 'Streak dan Konsistensi: Mainnya Jangka Panjang', 'Streak di Raga bukan soal sempurna tiap hari — tapi soal balik lagi setiap kali bolos.', $md$## Kenapa konsistensi menang

Perubahan berat badan yang bertahan lama hampir selalu datang dari kebiasaan kecil yang dilakukan berulang-ulang, bukan dari usaha heroik seminggu. Mencatat berat, jalan kaki rutin, tidur cukup — hal-hal membosankan inilah yang diam-diam mengubah grafik trenmu. Streak di Raga dirancang untuk merayakan hal membosankan itu.

## Bolos itu bukan gagal

Satu hari (atau satu minggu) terlewat tidak menghapus progresmu. Yang merusak bukan bolosnya, tapi keputusan untuk berhenti total karena merasa sudah gagal. Kalau streak-mu putus, tidak apa-apa. Catat lagi hari ini, dan streak barumu mulai lagi dari satu.

## Target proses, bukan cuma target hasil

Target hasil itu misalnya turun ke 65 kg. Target proses itu misalnya menimbang tiap Minggu pagi dan jalan 30 menit tiga kali seminggu. Target hasil tidak sepenuhnya dalam kendalimu, target proses iya. Raga membantumu di keduanya: target berat di tab Streak, dan streak menimbang sebagai target prosesmu.

## Cara pakai streak tanpa stres

Jadikan streak pengingat yang ramah, bukan mandor yang galak. Kalau melihat streak bikin kamu cemas, matikan dulu pengingatnya di Profil — tidak ada hukuman apa-apa. Raga tetap menyimpan datamu, dan kamu bisa balik kapan pun kamu siap. Hubungan yang sehat dengan data dimulai dari hubungan yang sehat dengan dirimu sendiri.

## Aturan jangan bolos dua kali

Ada aturan praktis yang populer di kalangan pembentuk kebiasaan: boleh bolos sekali, tapi jangan dua kali berturut-turut. Bolos sekali itu hidup — ada lembur, ada acara keluarga. Bolos dua kali mulai jadi pola baru. Aturan ini bukan hukum medis, cuma cara berpikir yang membantu banyak orang balik ke jalur tanpa drama.

## Rayakan yang kecil

Otakmu butuh bukti bahwa usahanya berarti. Delapan minggu streak, satu bulan mencatat rutin, pertama kali berani melihat grafik — itu semua layak dirayakan, meski angkanya belum ke mana-mana. Raga merayakan milestone-mu lewat Wrapped mingguan; kamu juga boleh merayakannya dengan caramu sendiri. Yang penting: jangan rayakan dengan berhenti.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'id') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('bmi-itu-apa', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'akademi-bmi' and locale = 'id'), 'BMI Itu Apa (dan Bukan Apa)', 'Indeks Massa Tubuh: rumus sederhana yang berguna untuk skrining — bukan rapor kesehatan pribadimu.', $md$## Rumusnya sederhana

BMI (Body Mass Index atau Indeks Massa Tubuh) adalah berat badan dalam kilogram dibagi kuadrat tinggi badan dalam meter. Satu rumus, tidak butuh alat mahal, bisa dihitung di mana saja. Justru kesederhanaan inilah yang membuatnya populer sekaligus terbatas.

## Kategorinya (untuk dewasa)

WHO memakai patokan ini untuk orang dewasa:

- Di bawah 18,5: berat badan kurang
- 18,5–24,9: normal
- 25–29,9: kelebihan berat badan
- 30 ke atas: obesitas

Patokan ini dibuat dari data populasi besar dan dipakai di seluruh dunia supaya datanya bisa dibandingkan antarnegara.

## Sejarah singkatnya

Rumus ini lahir tahun 1830-an dari matematikawan Belgia, Adolphe Quetelet. Awalnya ia tidak dirancang untuk menilai kesehatan individu, melainkan untuk mempelajari tubuh manusia rata-rata dalam populasi. Fakta ini penting: sejak awal BMI adalah alat statistik populasi.

## BMI itu untuk apa

BMI berguna sebagai langkah skrining pertama yang murah dan cepat. Buat peneliti dan pembuat kebijakan, ia memberi gambaran besar soal tren berat badan populasi. Buat kamu, ia adalah titik awal — sinyal untuk memperhatikan, bukan kesimpulan.

## BMI bukan untuk apa

BMI bukan diagnosis. Ia tidak mengukur lemak tubuhmu secara langsung, tidak tahu massa ototmu, dan tidak mempertimbangkan usiamu. Menentukan status kesehatan seseorang hanya dari BMI sama seperti menilai buku dari jumlah halamannya — ada informasinya, tapi ceritanya tidak lengkap.

## Kenapa masih dipakai sampai sekarang

Dengan segala keterbatasannya, BMI bertahan karena tiga hal: murah (tidak butuh alat), cepat (satu rumus), dan standar (datanya bisa dibandingkan antar daerah dan antar tahun). Untuk skrining awal dalam skala besar, kombinasi itu sulit dikalahkan. Masalahnya muncul kalau alat skrining dipakai sebagai vonis akhir.

## BMI di Raga

Di aplikasi ini, BMI adalah pintu masuk, bukan seluruh rumah. Raga menghitungnya otomatis, menampilkannya di gauge, lalu mengajakmu melihat lebih jauh: tren dari waktu ke waktu, estimasi kebutuhan kalori, dan artikel edukasi seperti yang sedang kamu baca ini. Anggap BMI sebagai kalimat pembuka percakapan tentang tubuhmu — bukan kesimpulan.$md$, 2, 'published', true, now(), now(), 'id') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('keterbatasan-bmi', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'akademi-bmi' and locale = 'id'), 'Keterbatasan BMI yang Wajib Kamu Tahu', 'BMI tidak bisa membedakan otot dan lemak — dan itu baru satu dari beberapa keterbatasannya.', $md$## Otot vs lemak: masalah terbesarnya

Satu kilogram otot dan satu kilogram lemak beratnya sama di timbangan, tapi BMI memperlakukan keduanya identik. Atlet atau orang yang rutin latihan kekuatan bisa masuk kategori kelebihan berat badan padahal kadar lemak tubuhnya rendah dan bugar. Sebaliknya, orang yang jarang bergerak bisa punya BMI normal tapi kadar lemak tubuhnya tinggi. BMI buta terhadap komposisi tubuh.

## Usia ikut bermain

Seiring bertambahnya usia, massa otot cenderung menurun sementara lemak tubuh bisa bertambah — dengan berat yang sama. Artinya angka BMI yang sama di usia 25 dan 65 bisa menceritakan kondisi yang berbeda. BMI juga tidak dirancang untuk ibu hamil atau anak-anak yang masih tumbuh.

## Tidak semua populasi sama

Penelitian menunjukkan hubungan antara BMI dan risiko kesehatan bisa berbeda antar kelompok etnis. Beberapa negara Asia, misalnya, memakai ambang batas yang sedikit lebih rendah karena risiko metabolik muncul pada BMI yang lebih kecil. Patokan global itu titik awal yang berguna, tapi bukan angka sakral yang sama untuk semua orang.

## Lemak perut tidak tertangkap

Dua orang dengan BMI identik bisa menyimpan lemak di tempat yang berbeda — dan lemak di sekitar perut (visceral) dikaitkan dengan risiko yang lebih tinggi dibanding lemak di pinggul atau paha. BMI tidak melihat distribusi ini sama sekali.

## Kesimpulannya

Pakai BMI bersama indikator lain: lingkar pinggang, kebugaran, tekanan darah, gula darah, dan yang terpenting, penilaian tenaga kesehatan profesional. Satu angka tidak pernah cukup untuk menyimpulkan kesehatan seseorang — termasuk kesehatanmu.

## Jadi harus pakai apa?

Jawaban jujurnya: kombinasi. BMI untuk gambaran kasar, lingkar pinggang untuk distribusi lemak, dan untuk gambaran yang lebih lengkap ada pemeriksaan klinis seperti tekanan darah, gula darah, dan profil lipid. Tidak ada satu angka ajaib yang merangkum kesehatanmu — dan justru itu kabar baiknya: kesehatanmu tidak ditentukan oleh satu angka pun.

## Cara berpikir yang lebih sehat

Daripada bertanya apakah BMI-mu normal, tanyakan: apakah kebiasaanku mendukung tubuhku? Apakah aku cukup bergerak, cukup tidur, makan dengan wajar? Angka bisa menyesatkan, tapi kebiasaan jarang berbohong. Fokus pada hal yang bisa kamu kendalikan hari ini.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'id') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('bmi-anak-remaja', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'akademi-bmi' and locale = 'id'), 'BMI untuk Anak dan Remaja: Jangan Pakai Kategori Dewasa', 'Tubuh anak masih tumbuh, jadi kategori BMI dewasa tidak berlaku. Yang dipakai adalah persentil BMI-menurut-usia.', $md$## Kenapa kategorinya beda

Kategori BMI dewasa (18,5 / 25 / 30) dibuat dari data orang dewasa yang tubuhnya sudah selesai tumbuh. Anak dan remaja masih dalam masa pertumbuhan: komposisi tubuh mereka berubah cepat mengikuti usia dan tahap pubertas. Menempelkan patokan dewasa ke tubuh yang masih tumbuh bukan cuma tidak akurat — tapi bisa menyesatkan.

## Yang dipakai: persentil BMI-menurut-usia

Untuk anak usia 2 tahun ke atas, profesional kesehatan memakai grafik pertumbuhan BMI-menurut-usia dan jenis kelamin. Hasilnya dibaca sebagai persentil: misalnya persentil ke-85 artinya BMI anak tersebut lebih tinggi dari 85 persen anak seusianya. Ini membandingkan anak dengan teman seusianya, bukan dengan orang dewasa.

## Jangan diagnosis sendiri

Grafik persentil adalah alat klinis. Membacanya butuh konteks: riwayat pertumbuhan anak, pola makan keluarga, aktivitas, dan faktor lainnya. Kalau kamu khawatir soal berat badan anakmu, langkah yang benar adalah diskusi dengan dokter anak atau ahli gizi — bukan menghakimi dari satu angka.

## Untuk orang tua: jaga bahasanya

Ini penting. Anak yang diejek soal berat badan — bahkan dengan niat bercanda — berisiko mengalami masalah citra tubuh dan pola makan yang tidak sehat di kemudian hari. Fokuskan percakapan pada kebiasaan keluarga yang sehat: makan bersama, aktif bergerak bareng, tidur cukup. Bukan pada angka timbangan anak.

## Kapan perlu ke profesional

Segera konsultasi kalau ada perubahan berat yang drastis dan tidak jelas sebabnya, atau kalau anak menunjukkan tanda gangguan pola makan. Deteksi dini selalu lebih baik, dan penanganannya adalah ranah tenaga profesional, bukan aplikasi.

## Yang bisa dilakukan keluarga

Lingkungan rumah punya pengaruh besar. Sediakan buah dan air putih yang gampang dijangkau, batasi minuman manis di rumah tanpa drama, ajak anak aktif bergerak dengan cara yang fun — main bola, sepedaan, jalan sore bareng. Tidur yang cukup juga penting karena kurang tidur dikaitkan dengan pola makan yang berantakan. Ini semua soal kebiasaan keluarga, bukan mengawasi piring anak dengan mikroskop.

## Fokus pada tumbuh, bukan menyusut

Bahasa yang dipakai orang dewasa di sekitar anak membentuk cara anak memandang tubuhnya sendiri. Hindari komentar soal bentuk tubuh siapa pun di depan anak, termasuk soal tubuhmu sendiri. Anak yang tumbuh dengan hubungan sehat terhadap makanan dan tubuhnya punya bekal yang jauh lebih berharga daripada angka persentil yang sempurna.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'id') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('otot-vs-lemak', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'akademi-bmi' and locale = 'id'), 'Otot vs Lemak: Kenapa Dua Orang dengan BMI Sama Bisa Beda Banget', 'Satu kilo otot dan satu kilo lemak beratnya sama — tapi cerita kesehatannya beda.', $md$## Ilustrasinya begini

Bayangkan dua orang: sama-sama 75 kg dan 175 cm, jadi BMI-nya sama persis, 24,5. Yang satu rutin angkat beban tiga kali seminggu, yang satu mager total. Angka timbangannya kembar, tapi yang satu badannya padat berotot dan yang satu lemak tubuhnya tinggi. Inilah titik buta BMI: ia melihat berat, bukan isinya.

## Kenapa atlet bisa kelebihan berat badan

Otot lebih padat daripada lemak — volumenya lebih kecil untuk berat yang sama. Orang yang berotot bisa dengan mudah masuk kategori kelebihan berat badan versi BMI padahal kadar lemaknya rendah dan kapasitas fisiknya bagus. Ini bukan BMI-nya yang rusak, ini memang bukan pekerjaan BMI sejak awal.

## Yang lebih penting dari angka timbangan

Kalau kamu latihan rutin, perhatikan hal lain selain timbangan: lingkar pinggang yang mengecil, baju yang lebih pas, napas yang tidak ngos-ngosan naik tangga, dan beban latihan yang pelan-pelan naik. Itu semua sinyal progres yang tidak bisa ditangkap satu angka BMI.

## Latihan kekuatan untuk pemula

Kabar baiknya, membangun otot tidak harus ke gym mewah. Dua sampai tiga sesi seminggu dengan beban tubuh sendiri — squat, push-up, plank — sudah cukup buat pemula. Mulai ringan, jaga teknik, tambah pelan-pelan. Otot yang bertambah juga membantu metabolisme dan postur tubuhmu sehari-hari.

## Catatannya

Artikel ini bukan anjuran program latihan medis. Kalau kamu punya kondisi kesehatan tertentu — jantung, sendi, atau sedang hamil — diskusikan dulu rencana latihanmu dengan tenaga profesional sebelum mulai.

## Timbangan naik padahal rajin latihan?

Ini jebakan klasik pemula: mulai latihan kekuatan, makan lebih teratur, tapi timbangan malah naik sekilo dua kilo. Jangan panik dulu. Di fase awal, tubuh bisa menambah massa otot sambil mengurangi lemak dalam waktu bersamaan — berat total bisa stagnan atau naik sedikit sementara komposisi tubuhmu sebenarnya membaik. Inilah kenapa cermin, meteran, dan baju yang lebih pas adalah juri yang lebih adil daripada timbangan sendirian.

## Sabar dengan prosesnya

Membentuk otot itu lambat — hitungannya bulan, bukan minggu. Tapi setiap sesi latihan adalah deposit. Selama lingkar pinggangmu tidak membesar dan kekuatanmu bertambah, kamu ada di jalur yang benar meski angka BMI-mu tidak bergerak ke mana-mana.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'id') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('lingkar-pinggang', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'akademi-bmi' and locale = 'id'), 'Lingkar Pinggang: Pelengkap BMI yang Murah dan Gampang', 'Lemak di sekitar perut berisiko lebih tinggi — dan mengukurnya cuma butuh meteran.', $md$## Kenapa perut jadi sorotan

Tidak semua lemak diciptakan sama. Lemak yang menumpuk di sekitar organ dalam perut (lemak visceral) dikaitkan dengan risiko gangguan metabolik yang lebih tinggi dibanding lemak di pinggul atau paha. BMI tidak bisa melihat di mana lemakmu disimpan — di sinilah lingkar pinggang membantu.

## Cara mengukur yang benar

Berdiri tegak dan rileks, lingkarkan meteran di titik tengah antara tulang rusuk terbawah dan puncak tulang panggul. Meterannya sejajar lantai, tidak menekan kulit, dan ukur setelah buang napas normal — bukan sambil menahan napas atau mengempiskan perut. Jujur saja, tidak ada yang menilai.

## Ambang batasnya

WHO memakai patokan untuk populasi dewasa: risiko mulai meningkat pada lingkar pinggang 94 cm ke atas untuk pria dan 80 cm ke atas untuk wanita, dengan risiko yang lebih tinggi lagi di 102 cm dan 88 cm. Ingat, ini patokan skrining populasi — tren angkamu dari waktu ke waktu sama pentingnya dengan angkanya sendiri.

## Bukan pengganti, tapi pelengkap

Lingkar pinggang tidak menggantikan BMI, ia melengkapinya. Kombinasi keduanya memberi gambaran yang lebih jujur: BMI bicara soal berat relatif terhadap tinggi, lingkar pinggang bicara soal distribusi lemak. Dua-duanya murah, cepat, dan bisa kamu lakukan di rumah.

## Kapan konsultasi

Kalau lingkar pinggangmu jauh di atas ambang batas dan bertahan di sana, atau naik terus selama berbulan-bulan, jadikan itu alasan untuk ngobrol dengan tenaga kesehatan profesional — bukan untuk panik. Data yang kamu kumpulkan justru membuat konsultasi itu jauh lebih produktif.

## Seberapa sering mengukur

Tidak perlu tiap hari — lingkar pinggang berubah jauh lebih lambat daripada berat badan. Sebulan sekali sudah cukup, di kondisi yang sama (pagi hari, sebelum makan). Catat angkanya di tempat yang sama dengan catatan beratmu supaya gampang dibandingkan.

## Catat bareng BMI

Kombinasi paling praktis buat kebanyakan orang: BMI dari Raga plus lingkar pinggang sebulan sekali. Kalau BMI-mu turun dan lingkar pinggangmu mengecil, itu sinyal kuat bahwa yang hilang memang lemak, bukan cuma air atau massa otot. Dua angka sederhana, cerita yang jauh lebih lengkap.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'id') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('kalori-itu-energi', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'nutrisi-energi' and locale = 'id'), 'Kalori Itu Energi, Bukan Musuh', 'Kalori sering diperlakukan seperti penjahat. Padahal kalori cuma satuan energi — yang penting bagaimana kamu mengelolanya dengan sadar.', $md$## Kalori itu sebenarnya apa?

Kalori itu cuma satuan energi, sama seperti kilometer untuk jarak atau liter untuk volume. Satu kilokalori (kkal, yang biasa ditulis kalori di label makanan) adalah jumlah energi yang dibutuhkan tubuhmu untuk beraktivitas. Jantung berdetak butuh kalori. Otak berpikir butuh kalori. Bahkan rebahan menonton serial pun membakar kalori.

Jadi kalori bukan racun, bukan musuh, bukan sesuatu yang harus dimusuhi. Tanpa kalori yang cukup, tubuhmu tidak bisa berfungsi.

## Dari mana kalori datang?

Ada tiga sumber utama, yang ahli gizi sebut makronutrien:

- **Karbohidrat** — sekitar 4 kkal per gram. Nasi, roti, mi, ubi, buah.
- **Protein** — sekitar 4 kkal per gram. Telur, ayam, ikan, tempe, tahu.
- **Lemak** — sekitar 9 kkal per gram. Minyak, santan, kacang-kacangan, alpukat.

Lemak kalorinya paling padat, makanya gorengan terasa berat di kalori walau porsinya kecil. Tapi ingat: padat kalori bukan berarti jahat. Lemak tetap dibutuhkan tubuh, misalnya untuk produksi hormon dan penyerapan vitamin.

## Kenapa kalori diperlakukan seperti penjahat?

Karena diet-diet populer membangun narasi kalori sama dengan musuh. Padahal yang memengaruhi berat badan bukan keberadaan kalori, melainkan **pola keseluruhan**: asupan yang terus-menerus melebihi kebutuhan dalam jangka panjang, minim gerak, kurang tidur, dan stres yang tidak dikelola.

Menyalahkan satu angka membuat orang terjebak di dua ekstrem: takut makan sampai kekurangan energi, atau sekalian masa bodoh. Keduanya tidak sehat.

## Terus harus bagaimana?

Kamu tidak perlu menghitung setiap butir nasi, kecuali kamu memang suka dan itu membantumu. Untuk kebanyakan orang, cukup lakukan ini:

1. **Pahami porsimu** — makan sampai cukup kenyang, bukan sampai begah.
2. **Utamakan makanan utuh** — yang bentuknya masih dikenali sebagai makanan, bukan produk ultra-olahan.
3. **Perhatikan minuman** — kalori cair seperti minuman manis dan kopi susu bergula sering tidak terasa tapi jumlahnya menumpuk.
4. **Konsisten, bukan sempurna** — satu hari makan berlebih tidak merusak apa-apa. Yang menentukan adalah polanya dalam berminggu-minggu.

Kalori adalah informasi, bukan vonis. Pakai informasinya untuk membuat keputusan yang lebih sadar, bukan untuk menghukum diri sendiri. Kalau kamu punya kondisi medis yang berkaitan dengan pola makan, diskusikan target asupanmu dengan dokter atau ahli gizi.$md$, 2, 'published', true, now(), now(), 'id') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('bmr-tdee-bahasa-manusia', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'nutrisi-energi' and locale = 'id'), 'BMR dan TDEE Dijelaskan dengan Bahasa Manusia', 'Dua singkatan ini muncul di mana-mana soal kalori. Ini penjelasan sederhananya: apa artinya, dan cara memakainya tanpa jadi budak angka.', $md$## BMR: biaya hidup tubuhmu

BMR (Basal Metabolic Rate) adalah perkiraan energi yang dibakar tubuhmu dalam sehari **kalau kamu hanya rebahan total selama 24 jam** — untuk napas, detak jantung, kerja otak, dan menjaga suhu tubuh. Angkanya dipengaruhi berat badan, tinggi, umur, dan jenis kelamin.

Rumus populer seperti Mifflin-St Jeor dipakai banyak aplikasi, termasuk Raga, untuk mengestimasi BMR. Penting digarisbawahi: ini **estimasi dari rumus umum**, bukan hasil pengukuran laboratorium atas tubuhmu.

## TDEE: total pengeluaran harianmu

TDEE (Total Daily Energy Expenditure) adalah BMR ditambah seluruh aktivitasmu: jalan ke warung, naik tangga, olahraga, bahkan kebiasaan menggoyang-goyangkan kaki. Karena itu TDEE dihitung dengan mengalikan BMR dengan faktor aktivitas, dari yang sangat jarang bergerak sampai yang sangat aktif.

Angka inilah yang dipakai sebagai patokan kebutuhan kalori harianmu.

## Kenapa dua orang bisa beda jauh?

Karena komposisi tubuh dan aktivitasnya beda. Orang dengan massa otot lebih besar biasanya BMR-nya lebih tinggi, karena otot itu mahal untuk dirawat tubuh. Orang yang bekerja fisik seharian bisa punya TDEE dua kali lipat pekerja kantoran dengan BMR yang mirip.

Jadi jangan bandingkan kebutuhan kalorimu dengan orang lain. Bandingkan dengan **tren tubuhmu sendiri** dari waktu ke waktu.

## Cara memakai angka ini dengan waras

1. **Jadikan titik awal, bukan kitab suci.** Mulai dari estimasi TDEE, lalu amati tren berat badan selama 2 sampai 4 minggu.
2. **Ubah pelan-pelan.** Kalau targetmu menurunkan berat, defisit kecil, misalnya 300 sampai 500 kkal di bawah TDEE, jauh lebih bisa dipertahankan daripada potongan brutal.
3. **Jangan makan jauh di bawah BMR terus-menerus** tanpa pengawasan profesional. Itu resep badan lemas dan metabolisme yang beradaptasi ke arah yang tidak kamu mau.
4. **Hitung ulang berkala.** Berat berubah, BMR ikut berubah. Perbarui estimasimu setiap beberapa bulan.

Rumus itu alat bantu, bukan diagnosis. Kalau kamu punya kondisi medis seperti gangguan tiroid atau diabetes, diskusikan target kalorimu dengan dokter atau ahli gizi, jangan hanya mengandalkan angka aplikasi.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'id') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('protein-fondasi', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'nutrisi-energi' and locale = 'id'), 'Protein: Fondasi yang Paling Sering Kurang', 'Bukan cuma buat yang mau berotot. Protein adalah bahan bangunan tubuh dan makronutrien paling mengenyangkan — dan banyak orang kurang memenuhinya.', $md$## Protein itu untuk apa saja?

Banyak yang mengira protein hanya untuk orang yang mau berotot. Padahal protein adalah bahan bangunan hampir semua hal di tubuhmu: otot, kulit, rambut, enzim pencernaan, hormon, sampai sistem imun. Kekurangan protein dalam jangka panjang membuat pemulihan melambat, tubuh gampang sakit, dan massa otot menyusut, terutama saat berat badan sedang turun.

Kabar baiknya: protein juga makronutrien **paling mengenyangkan**. Makanan berprotein membuatmu kenyang lebih lama dibanding jumlah kalori yang sama dari karbohidrat olahan.

## Butuh berapa banyak?

Angka yang sering dipakai sebagai patokan umum adalah sekitar **0,8 gram per kilogram berat badan per hari** untuk orang dewasa dengan aktivitas ringan. Orang yang aktif berolahraga biasanya butuh lebih, tetapi penentuan pastinya lebih baik dibahas dengan ahli.

Ini patokan populasi umum, bukan resep personal. Ibu hamil, ibu menyusui, lansia, dan orang dengan penyakit ginjal punya kebutuhan yang berbeda. Untuk kondisi khusus, konsultasikan dengan dokter atau ahli gizi.

## Sumber protein yang gampang dicari

Tidak harus dada ayam rebus tawar setiap hari:

- **Hewani:** telur, ayam, ikan seperti kembung, tongkol, dan lele yang murah dan mudah didapat, daging sapi, susu dan yogurt.
- **Nabati:** tempe, tahu, edamame, kacang-kacangan. Tempe itu juara lokal: murah, proteinnya padat, dan proses fermentasinya baik untuk pencernaan.

Kombinasikan keduanya supaya tidak bosan dan dompet tetap aman.

## Perlu khawatir soal protein nabati yang tidak lengkap?\n\nProtein hewani umumnya mengandung semua asam amino esensial dalam proporsi yang baik, sementara kebanyakan protein nabati kekurangan satu atau dua jenis. Kabar baiknya: kamu tidak perlu melengkapinya dalam satu piring. Selama dalam sehari kamu makan bervariasi, misalnya tempe di siang hari dan telur di malam hari, tubuhmu sudah mendapatkan semuanya. Jadi pola makan nabati pun bisa memenuhi kebutuhan protein dengan perencanaan sederhana, tanpa stres berlebihan.\n\n## Cara simpel menambah protein

- Tambahkan **satu sumber protein di setiap makan besar**. Makanmu selama ini nasi plus sayur saja? Tambah telur atau tempe.
- Sarapan berprotein, misalnya telur atau yogurt, membuat keinginan ngemil di siang hari berkurang drastis bagi banyak orang.
- Ganti camilan: kacang rebus atau edamame sebagai pengganti keripik.

Tidak perlu bubuk suplemen mahal untuk mulai. Makanan sehari-hari dengan porsi yang benar sudah membawa perubahan besar.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'id') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('serat-kenyang-lebih-lama', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'nutrisi-energi' and locale = 'id'), 'Serat: Cara Paling Gampang Kenyang Lebih Lama', 'Serat tidak dicerna tubuh, tapi justru itu kelebihannya: menambah volume makanan, melambatkan lapar, dan menyehatkan pencernaan.', $md$## Serat itu apa?

Serat adalah bagian dari makanan nabati yang **tidak dicerna** oleh tubuhmu. Kedengarannya sia-sia, tapi justru di situlah kelebihannya: serat menambah volume makanan tanpa menambah kalori berarti, melambatkan proses pencernaan, dan menjadi makanan bagi bakteri baik di ususmu.

## Kenapa serat bikin kenyang lebih lama?

Ada tiga mekanisme sederhana:

1. **Volume** — makanan berserat mengambil ruang di lambung, sehingga sinyal kenyang datang lebih cepat.
2. **Dicerna perlahan** — gula darah naiknya landai, tidak melonjak lalu anjlok seperti setelah minuman manis, yang biasanya membuatmu lapar lagi sejam kemudian.
3. **Butuh dikunyah** — makan jadi lebih pelan, dan otak sempat sadar bahwa kamu sudah kenyang.

Itulah kenapa semangkuk sayur plus protein membuatmu kenyang berjam-jam, sementara jumlah kalori yang sama dari minuman manis hilang entah ke mana.

## Sumber serat sehari-hari

- Sayur: bayam, kangkung, brokoli, wortel. Tambahkan satu genggam setiap kali makan.
- Buah utuh, bukan jus: pepaya, pisang, apel, jeruk.
- Kacang-kacangan dan biji-bijian: kacang merah, kacang hijau.
- Karbohidrat utuh: nasi merah, oatmeal, jagung, ubi.

Patokan umum yang sering dikutip adalah sekitar **25 sampai 30 gram per hari** untuk orang dewasa. Kenyataannya, kebanyakan orang masih jauh di bawah angka itu.

## Serat larut vs tidak larut, perlu tahu?\n\nSerat larut, misalnya pada oatmeal, apel, dan kacang-kacangan, larut dalam air membentuk gel yang melambatkan pencernaan serta membantu mengontrol gula darah dan kolesterol. Serat tidak larut, misalnya pada sayuran hijau, kulit buah, dan gandum utuh, menambah massa feses dan melancarkan buang air besar. Kamu tidak perlu menghafal daftarnya. Cukup makan bervariasi dari kedua kelompok setiap hari, dan tubuhmu mendapatkan manfaat keduanya.\n\n## Naikkan bertahap, dan jangan lupa air

Jangan lompat dari nol langsung ke 30 gram — perutmu akan protes dengan kembung dan begah. Tambahkan bertahap selama satu sampai dua minggu, dan **minum air yang cukup**, karena serat membutuhkan air untuk bekerja dengan baik. Serat tanpa air yang cukup justru bisa menyebabkan sembelit.

Aturan praktisnya sederhana: setengah piring berisi sayur dan buah setiap makan besar. Murah, mudah, dan efeknya terasa dalam hitungan hari.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'id') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('diet-ekstrem-vs-pola-makan', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'nutrisi-energi' and locale = 'id'), 'Diet Ekstrem vs Pola Makan yang Bisa Dipertahankan', 'Diet ekstrem menjanjikan hasil cepat, tapi buktinya justru merusak: otot hilang, berat yo-yo, dan hubungan dengan makanan jadi berantakan. Ini alternatif yang masuk akal.', $md$## Kenapa diet ekstrem begitu menggoda?

Janji turun banyak kilogram dalam dua minggu itu candu. Diet ekstrem — puasa total berhari-hari, hanya makan satu jenis makanan, atau kalori super rendah tanpa pengawasan — menawarkan jalan pintas. Dan jalan pintas selalu laku.

Masalahnya: yang turun cepat di minggu-minggu awal sebagian besar adalah **air dan massa otot**, bukan lemak. Begitu diet berhenti, berat naik lagi, seringkali lebih tinggi dari sebelumnya. Inilah yang disebut efek yo-yo, dan polanya terdokumentasi dengan baik dalam literatur kesehatan.

## Kenapa diet ekstrem berbahaya?

Bagian ini penting, jadi dibaca pelan-pelan:

- **Yang hilang otot, bukan cuma lemak.** Defisit ekstrem membuat tubuh membongkar otot untuk energi. Otot itu susah dibangun dan gampang hilang.
- **Tubuh beradaptasi.** Pengeluaran energimu bisa menurun sebagai respons terhadap kelaparan berkepanjangan. Itu sebabnya berat mentok, lalu melonjak lagi saat kamu makan normal.
- **Kekurangan gizi.** Menghapus kelompok makanan besar-besaran tanpa pengganti berarti defisiensi vitamin dan mineral.
- **Hubungan rusak dengan makanan.** Aturan kaku, rasa bersalah setiap meleset sedikit, siklus menahan lalu kalap makan. Kalau kamu merasa kehilangan kontrol atas pola makanmu, **cari bantuan profesional**. Itu bukan kelemahan, itu langkah berani.
- **Risiko medis serius** pada diet sangat rendah kalori tanpa pengawasan, termasuk gangguan irama jantung, batu empedu, dan ketidakseimbangan elektrolit.

## Ciri diet yang patut dicurigai

- Menjanjikan hasil cepat dan pasti.
- Melarang total kelompok makanan tanpa alasan medis.
- Mewajibkan membeli produk atau suplemen tertentu.
- Aturannya kaku: sedikit meleset dianggap gagal total.
- Tidak pernah menyarankan konsultasi dengan profesional kesehatan.

## Alternatifnya: pola makan seimbang

Bukan program 30 hari, melainkan **pola makan**: cara kamu makan sehari-hari, tahun demi tahun.

1. **Defisit kecil dan sabar.** Turun sekitar 0,5 kg per minggu adalah kecepatan yang sehat dan bisa dipertahankan bagi kebanyakan orang.
2. **Tidak ada makanan yang dilarang total.** Yang ada adalah porsi dan frekuensi.
3. **Protein dan serat cukup** agar kenyang dan massa otot terjaga.
4. **Fleksibel.** Ada acara keluarga atau liburan? Nikmati, lalu kembali ke pola normal. Satu hari tidak merusak apa-apa.
5. **Didampingi profesional** jika kamu punya kondisi medis.

Tubuhmu bukan proyek 30 hari. Bangunlah cara makan yang masih kamu jalani lima tahun dari sekarang. Itulah yang benar-benar berhasil.$md$, 3, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'id') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('tidur-dan-berat-badan', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'kebiasaan-sehat' and locale = 'id'), 'Tidur dan Berat Badan: Hubungan yang Diremehkan', 'Kurang tidur mengacaukan hormon lapar dan membuatmu memilih makanan berkalori tinggi. Memperbaiki tidur bisa jadi langkah paling underrated dalam perjalananmu.', $md$## Begadang bikin besoknya lapar terus

Ini bukan sekadar perasaan, ada mekanismenya. Kurang tidur mengacaukan dua hormon pengatur nafsu makan: **ghrelin** si pemicu lapar naik, sementara **leptin** si pemberi sinyal kenyang turun. Hasilnya: kamu merasa lapar padahal tubuhmu tidak membutuhkan energi tambahan.

Riset juga menunjukkan orang yang kurang tidur cenderung memilih makanan tinggi kalori dalam porsi lebih besar. Otak yang lelah sulit berkata cukup.

## Keputusan makan memburuk saat lelah

Coba ingat-ingat: kapan terakhir kamu begadang lalu berakhir memesan gorengan jam sebelas malam? Saat lelah, bagian otak yang berpikir jangka panjang melemah, sementara pusat reward jadi lebih reaktif terhadap makanan manis dan berlemak. Ini bukan soal kurang niat, memang biologinya bekerja begitu.

## Berapa jam yang cukup?

Rekomendasi umum untuk orang dewasa adalah **7 sampai 9 jam per malam**. Ini patokan populasi, bukan vonis personal, karena kebutuhan tiap orang sedikit berbeda. Remaja butuh lebih banyak, sekitar 8 sampai 10 jam. Kalau kamu rutin tidur di bawah 6 jam dan siang hari terus mengantuk, itu sinyal yang jelas untuk berubah.

## Tidur juga soal pemulihan

Saat tidur nyenyak, tubuh memperbaiki otot, mengatur hormon, dan mengonsolidasikan memori, termasuk memori kebiasaan baik yang kamu bangun di siang hari. Olahraga tanpa tidur yang cukup berarti pemulihan yang setengah-setengah.

## Cara tidur lebih baik tanpa beli alat mahal

- **Jam yang konsisten.** Bangun di jam yang sama setiap hari, termasuk akhir pekan. Ini strategi yang paling powerful.
- **Kamar gelap, sejuk, dan sepi.** Tubuh butuh sinyal yang jelas bahwa waktunya istirahat.
- **Kurangi layar 30 sampai 60 menit sebelum tidur**, atau setidaknya hindari konten yang bikin emosi.
- **Batasi kafein** setelah lewat tengah hari. Efeknya bisa bertahan 6 sampai 8 jam.
- **Hindari olahraga sangat intens mepet jam tidur** kalau itu membuatmu sulit terlelap.

Satu catatan penting: kalau kamu mendengkur keras, seperti berhenti bernapas saat tidur, atau mengantuk berat di siang hari terus-menerus, itu bisa jadi tanda sleep apnea atau gangguan tidur lain. **Periksakan ke dokter** dan jangan mendiagnosis sendiri.$md$, 2, 'published', true, now(), now(), 'id') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('jalan-kaki-underrated', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'kebiasaan-sehat' and locale = 'id'), 'Jalan Kaki: Olahraga Paling Underrated', 'Nol hambatan, bisa dilakukan siapa saja, dan efeknya nyata untuk jantung, berat badan, dan stres. Ini panduan memulainya dengan waras.', $md$## Kenapa jalan kaki begitu powerful?

Karena hambatannya nol: tidak perlu gym, tidak perlu skill khusus, tidak perlu outfit tertentu. Tapi efeknya nyata: jalan kaki rutin memperbaiki kesehatan jantung, membantu pengelolaan berat badan, menstabilkan gula darah, dan yang sering dilupakan, **menurunkan stres**.

Jalan kaki juga termasuk NEAT, singkatan dari Non-Exercise Activity Thermogenesis, yaitu energi yang terbakar dari aktivitas sehari-hari di luar olahraga formal. Bagi kebanyakan orang, NEAT justru penyumbang terbesar perbedaan pengeluaran energi harian. Artinya, orang yang banyak berjalan dalam kesehariannya bisa membakar jauh lebih banyak daripada orang yang hanya olahraga 30 menit lalu duduk sepuluh jam.

## Tidak harus langsung 10.000 langkah

Angka 10.000 itu awalnya slogan marketing pedometer, bukan titah medis. Yang penting adalah **mulai dari posisimu sekarang, lalu naikkan bertahap**.

- Di bawah 3.000 langkah per hari? Targetkan 5.000 dulu.
- Sudah 5.000? Naik ke 7.000 sampai 8.000.
- Tambah 500 sampai 1.000 langkah per minggu adalah progres yang bagus.

Konsisten di 7.000 langkah setiap hari mengalahkan 15.000 langkah yang hanya kejadian dua kali sebulan.

## Cara menyelipkan jalan kaki ke harimu

- Turun dari transportasi umum satu halte lebih awal.
- Teleponan sambil berjalan, bukan sambil duduk.
- Parkir di tempat paling jauh. Gratis dan sehat.
- Jalan 10 menit setelah makan. Bonusnya: membantu gula darah juga.
- Ajak teman atau dengarkan podcast supaya tidak bosan.

## Mulai minggu ini\n\nTidak perlu rencana rumit. Pilih satu pemicu yang sudah ada: jalan sepuluh menit setelah makan siang, atau jalan lima belas menit sepulang kerja. Lakukan lima hari minggu ini, lalu catat di Raga atau kalendermu. Minggu depan tambah lima menit. Kemajuan kecil yang tercatat jauh lebih memotivasi daripada target besar yang tidak pernah dimulai.\n\n## Jalan kaki vs lari, mana menang?

Keduanya baik. Lari membakar lebih banyak per menit, tetapi bebannya ke sendi lebih besar dan lebih banyak orang yang menyerah di minggu kedua. Jalan kaki bisa dilakukan hampir semua orang, hampir setiap hari, selama bertahun-tahun.

Olahraga terbaik bukan yang paling keren, melainkan yang **masih kamu lakukan enam bulan dari sekarang**. Kalau kamu punya kondisi sendi, jantung, atau medis lain, diskusikan dulu pilihan olahragamu dengan dokter.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'id') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('konsistensi-vs-intensitas', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'kebiasaan-sehat' and locale = 'id'), 'Konsistensi Mengalahkan Intensitas', 'Semangat 3 hari lalu menyerah total adalah pola klasik. Kebiasaan kecil yang bertahan setahun mengalahkan program heroik yang cuma bertahan seminggu.', $md$## Jebakan semangat tiga hari

Pola yang familiar: Senin mulai diet ketat plus olahraga dua jam. Rabu masih jalan. Jumat sudah pesan martabak. Minggu menyerah total, menunggu Senin depan untuk mulai lagi.

Masalahnya bukan niatmu, melainkan **strateginya**. Perubahan raksasa butuh energi raksasa, dan energi itu cepat habis. Yang tersisa biasanya cuma rasa bersalah.

## Matematika kebiasaan kecil

Perbedaan satu persen setiap hari terdengar remeh, tetapi perilaku itu berbunga seperti tabungan:

- Jalan 20 menit tiap hari selama setahun sama dengan **lebih dari 120 jam** gerak.
- Mengurangi satu minuman manis sehari berarti **puluhan ribu kkal** dalam setahun yang tidak masuk.
- Menimbang seminggu sekali berarti **52 data point** untuk membaca trenmu.

Tidak ada satu pun yang heroik. Semuanya bisa dipertahankan. Itulah poinnya.

## Cara membuat kebiasaan menempel

1. **Kecil sampai terasa konyol.** Target olahraga 30 menit selalu gagal? Turunkan jadi pakai sepatu olahraga dan jalan lima menit. Ambang untuk mulai harus serendah mungkin.
2. **Spesifik.** Bukan makan lebih sehat, melainkan tambah sayur di makan siang setiap hari kerja.
3. **Tempel ke rutinitas lama.** Contoh: setelah sikat gigi malam, siapkan botol minum untuk besok. Kebiasaan baru paling mudah menempel pada kebiasaan lama.
4. **Catat.** Dicentang di kalender atau dicatat di Raga. Progres yang terlihat membuat otak ketagihan untuk lanjut.

## Lingkungan mengalahkan tekad\n\nTekad itu sumber daya yang habis dipakai, sedangkan lingkungan bekerja tanpa kamu sadari. Taruh sepatu olahraga di depan pintu, simpan camilan manis di tempat yang sulit dijangkau, siapkan bekal sejak malam sebelumnya. Orang yang konsisten biasanya bukan yang tekadnya paling kuat, melainkan yang paling pandai mendesain hari-harinya supaya pilihan yang baik menjadi pilihan yang termudah.\n\n## Satu hari bolos bukan kegagalan

Riset tentang kebiasaan menunjukkan yang membuat orang berhasil bukan kesempurnaan, melainkan **kecepatan untuk kembali**. Bolos sehari? Lanjut besok seolah tidak terjadi apa-apa. Yang berbahaya bukan bolosnya, melainkan cerita ah sudahlah, gagal total yang membuat bolos sehari menjadi bolos sebulan.

Targetnya bukan seratus hari sempurna. Targetnya sederhana: **jangan pernah bolos dua kali berturut-turut**.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'id') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('cara-menimbang-yang-benar', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'kebiasaan-sehat' and locale = 'id'), 'Cara Menimbang yang Benar Biar Datanya Jujur', 'Banyak orang menimbang asal-asalan lalu panik atau senang berdasarkan angka yang sebenarnya cuma noise. Ini cara mendapatkan data yang bisa dibandingkan.', $md$## Timbangan hanya sebagus caramu memakainya

Banyak orang menimbang asal-asalan: beda jam, beda pakaian, habis makan besar, lalu panik atau senang berdasarkan angka yang sebenarnya hanya noise. Padahal yang kamu butuhkan bukan angka sempurna, melainkan **angka yang bisa dibandingkan** dari waktu ke waktu.

## Aturan mainnya

1. **Waktu yang sama.** Pagi hari setelah bangun dan buang air kecil, sebelum makan atau minum. Ini kondisi paling konsisten.
2. **Kondisi yang sama.** Pakaian yang minimal atau sama setiap kali. Jangan bandingkan pagi berpakaian kaos dengan malam berpakaian jeans.
3. **Timbangan dan permukaan yang sama.** Lantai keras dan rata. Pindah-pindah timbangan berarti pindah-pindah angka.
4. **Frekuensi yang kamu sanggup.** Seminggu sekali cukup untuk kebanyakan orang. Setiap hari boleh kalau kamu tipe yang tidak baperan dengan fluktuasi. Kalau angka harian membuatmu stres, pilih mingguan saja.

## Jangan panik dengan fluktuasi harian

Berat badan bisa naik turun **0,5 sampai 2 kg dalam sehari**. Penyebabnya normal semua: air minum, makanan yang belum dicerna, garam yang menahan air, kurang tidur, stres, siklus menstruasi, dan bagi yang berolahraga, peradangan otot setelah latihan.

Naik satu kilogram besok pagi bukan berarti lemakmu bertambah satu kilogram. Dibutuhkan surplus sekitar 7.700 kkal untuk menambah satu kilogram lemak. Hampir mustahil terjadi dalam semalam.

## Yang penting: tren, bukan titik

Lihat rata-rata mingguan atau tren dua sampai empat minggu. Naik turun harian itu cuaca, tren itu iklim. Raga membuat grafik tren justru untuk ini: supaya kamu membaca iklim, bukan panik karena cuaca.

## Kapan timbangan bukan alat yang tepat?

- **Masa kehamilan.** Berat naik itu normal dan diperlukan. Targetnya dibahas dengan dokter atau bidan.
- **Riwayat gangguan makan.** Angka bisa menjadi pemicu. Fokus pada kebiasaan dengan pendampingan profesional.
- **Kondisi medis tertentu** seperti gagal jantung atau gangguan ginjal, di mana fluktuasi berat bisa menjadi sinyal medis. Ikuti panduan dokternya.

Timbangan itu alat ukur, bukan hakim. Dipakai dengan benar, dia jadi teman. Dipakai dengan salah, dia jadi sumber stres gratisan.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'id') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('mindful-eating', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'kebiasaan-sehat' and locale = 'id'), 'Mindful Eating Tanpa Drama', 'Bukan diet dan bukan aturan kaku. Mindful eating cuma soal hadir saat makan — dan itu cukup untuk mengubah banyak hal.', $md$## Apa itu mindful eating?

Mindful eating artinya makan dengan perhatian penuh. Bukan diet, bukan aturan boleh versus tidak boleh, bukan ritual ribet. Sederhananya: **hadir saat makan**. Rasakan rasa, tekstur, dan sinyal kenyang tubuhmu.

Kedengarannya sepele sampai kamu sadar betapa sering kita makan sambil scroll HP, menonton, atau bekerja, lalu tiba-tiba piring kosong dan kita tidak ingat rasanya.

## Kenapa makan sambil scroll bikin kebablasan?

Otakmu butuh sekitar **20 menit** untuk menerima sinyal kenyang dari lambung. Kalau perhatianmu ada di layar, kamu makan lebih cepat dan sinyal itu terlewat. Hasilnya: porsi yang sama terasa kurang, padahal tubuhmu sebenarnya sudah cukup.

Ini bukan soal disiplin, melainkan soal desain perhatian. Ubah desainnya, perilakunya ikut berubah.

## Empat langkah simpel untuk mulai

1. **Singkirkan layar** saat makan, minimal untuk satu kali makan sehari dulu. Tidak harus semuanya sekaligus.
2. **Makan lebih pelan.** Letakkan sendok di antara suapan. Kunyah dengan benar. Terdengar konyol, efeknya nyata.
3. **Gunakan skala kenyang 1 sampai 10.** Sebelum makan tanya ke diri sendiri: laparku level berapa? Di tengah makan: sekarang berapa? Berhentilah di sekitar 7 sampai 8, yaitu kenyang nyaman, bukan 10 yang begah.
4. **Bedakan lapar fisik dan lapar emosi.** Lapar fisik datang bertahap dan menerima banyak jenis makanan. Lapar emosi datang tiba-tiba dan mengidam satu hal spesifik, biasanya yang manis atau gurih. Kalau yang terjadi yang kedua, coba jeda sepuluh menit dan segelas air dulu.

## Bukan soal sempurna

Mindful eating bukan berarti setiap suapan harus jadi meditasi. Makan sambil mengobrol bersama keluarga juga mindful, karena perhatianmu ada di momennya. Targetnya bukan kesempurnaan ritual, melainkan **lebih sering hadir** daripada autopilot.

Satu batas yang penting: kalau hubunganmu dengan makanan sudah sampai di titik yang mengganggu, misalnya makan berlebihan yang tidak terkendali, rasa bersalah yang ekstrem, atau pola berbahaya lainnya, itu sudah di luar wilayah artikel ini. **Cari bantuan profesional.** Tidak ada tips internet yang bisa menggantikannya.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'id') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('kenalan-dengan-raga', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'memahami-raga' and locale = 'en'), 'Getting to Know Raga: A 5-Minute Tour', 'A quick guide to using Raga: from logging your first weight to reading trends and keeping your streak.', $md$## What does Raga actually do?

Raga is a BMI calculator with history, trend charts, and weigh-in reminders. The idea is simple: you just log your weight regularly, and Raga does the math, stores the data, and presents it in a way that's easy to read. No drama, no judgment.

## The Calculate tab: your starting point

This is where it all begins. Enter your height and weight, tap Calculate BMI, and you instantly get your result: your BMI number, a colored gauge, and its category. Want to save it? Tap Save to history. You can also create multiple profiles — for family members, say — and switch the active profile with the chips at the top.

## The History tab: where the story lives

A single scale reading tells you nothing, but a string of readings over weeks tells you a lot. In the History tab you can see your weight chart, switch time ranges (7 days to all time), and read automatic insights like averages, upward or downward trends, and estimated weekly pace.

## The Health tab: the numbers behind the scenes

This tab calculates three things from your data: BMR (the energy your body burns at complete rest), daily calorie needs based on activity level, and the ideal weight range for your height. Important: these are all ESTIMATES from general formulas, not medical verdicts. Treat them as a starting point for discussion, not a final conclusion.

## The Streak, Arena, and Profile tabs

Streak tracks your weekly weigh-in consistency and the weight goal you've set. Arena is a community leaderboard for logged-in users who opt in (opt-in defaults to off — your data stays private). Profile holds your unit, theme, reminder, and cloud backup settings.

## Tips for honest data

Weigh yourself under the same conditions every time: in the morning, after using the bathroom, before eating, in similar clothing. Log regularly, and don't get emotional over one weird reading — look at the trend.

## Your data is yours

Raga has no ads and doesn't sell data. All your entries are stored on your phone first, and the app works fully offline. If you want, there's an optional cloud backup to keep your data safe when you switch phones, complete with a backup code that only you hold.

## Where to start?

If this is your first time opening the app: finish onboarding (name, height, starting weight), log one weigh-in to get your first number, and don't rush to set a goal. Spend a week getting a feel for the rhythm. You can set a weight goal later in the Streak tab when you're ready, and change it anytime.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'en') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('cara-baca-gauge-bmi', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'memahami-raga' and locale = 'en'), 'How to Read the BMI Gauge Without Panicking', 'What the colors and numbers on Raga''s BMI gauge mean — plus one important thing: BMI is a screening tool, not a diagnosis.', $md$## Where does the number come from?

The gauge in Raga shows your Body Mass Index (BMI): your weight in kilograms divided by the square of your height in meters. For example, 70 kg at 1.70 m gives 70 divided by (1.70 × 1.70), about 24.2. Raga calculates this automatically every time you log.

## The colors and categories

The gauge uses the WHO classification for adults:

- Below 18.5: underweight
- 18.5–24.9: normal
- 25–29.9: overweight
- 30 and above: obesity

Each range has its own color so you can read it at a glance. These categories are population screening benchmarks, not labels stamped on your forehead.

## What the gauge does NOT tell you

This is the most important part. The gauge doesn't know your body composition — it can't tell muscle from fat. It doesn't know your age, your health history, or the fat around your belly. Two people with the same gauge number can be in very different shape.

## How to use the gauge healthily

Use the gauge as a compass, not a judge. If the number sits outside the normal range and stays there for weeks, that's a signal to dig deeper — read the BMI limitations article in BMI Academy, for instance, or talk to a health professional. One weird reading now and then? Ignore it, watch the trend.

## A way to think about it

Say the gauge shows 27, the overweight category. The healthy response isn't panic or an instant crash diet. Instead: first, check whether this number is consistent over several weeks or just an occasional blip. Second, look at the bigger picture — waist circumference, how active you are, health history. Third, if the number persists, use it as material for a conversation with a professional.

## When the number deserves to be taken seriously

One category above normal is not a verdict, but if your BMI is 30 or above and stays there, or keeps climbing for months, it deserves to be taken seriously. Not by blaming yourself, but by getting the right help: a doctor, a nutritionist, or simply starting with the small habits covered in the Healthy Habits category.$md$, 2, 'published', true, now(), now(), 'en') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('membaca-grafik-tren', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'memahami-raga' and locale = 'en'), 'Reading the Trend Chart: One Number Tells You Nothing', 'Daily weight ups and downs are normal. What matters is the direction of the trend, not today''s number.', $md$## Why daily numbers lie

Your weight can swing 1–2 kg in a single day, and that's normal. The causes are trivial things: drinking a lot, salty food last night, digestion, or for some people, hormonal cycles. None of that means you suddenly got fatter or thinner in 24 hours.

## The rules of weighing

To make your data comparable, keep conditions consistent: weigh in the morning, after the bathroom, before eating or drinking, in similar clothing. You don't have to do it every day — once a week, same day and time, is good enough for most people.

## How to read the chart in Raga

In the History tab, switch the time range and watch the direction of the line, not the individual dots. A line slowly descending over weeks means your effort is working, even if some dots spike upward along the way. Raga also shows averages and weekly pace so you don't have to guess.

## When to be cautious

Watch out for drastic, unintentional changes — for example, weight dropping or rising a lot in a short time with no change in eating or activity. That's not about dieting; it's a signal to consult a health professional. A healthy trend is slow and steady, not dramatic.

## Weekly averages: the simple trick

If daily numbers make you overthink, try this: weigh three times a week under the same conditions, then look at the average. A weekly average is far more stable and honest than any single daily reading. Compare this week's average with last week's — that's where the real trend is.

## Don't chase the daily number

The most common mistake: seeing the scale up almost a kilo this morning and punishing yourself by barely eating all day. That's like driving while staring at the rear-view mirror — your reactions are always late and pointed the wrong way. Decisions about eating should come from weekly trends, not one number this morning.

## Keep the context too

A number without context is easy to misread. If you were sick, sleep-deprived, or had a week full of food events, note it — Raga stores your history as it is, and context helps you re-read the chart months later without blaming yourself for a messy week.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'en') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('streak-dan-konsistensi', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'memahami-raga' and locale = 'en'), 'Streaks and Consistency: Play the Long Game', 'Streaks in Raga aren''t about being perfect every day — they''re about coming back every time you slip.', $md$## Why consistency wins

Lasting weight change almost always comes from small habits repeated over and over, not from one heroic week. Logging your weight, walking regularly, sleeping enough — these boring things quietly reshape your trend chart. The Streak in Raga is designed to celebrate that boring stuff.

## Missing a day isn't failure

One day (or one week) missed doesn't erase your progress. What destroys it isn't the miss — it's deciding to quit entirely because you feel you've failed. If your streak breaks, that's fine. Log again today, and your new streak starts at one.

## Process goals, not just outcome goals

An outcome goal is, say, getting down to 65 kg. A process goal is weighing every Sunday morning and walking 30 minutes three times a week. The outcome isn't fully in your control; the process is. Raga helps with both: weight goals in the Streak tab, and the weigh-in streak as your process goal.

## How to use streaks without stress

Treat the streak as a friendly reminder, not a strict boss. If looking at your streak makes you anxious, turn off the reminders in Profile for a while — there's no punishment. Raga keeps your data, and you can come back whenever you're ready. A healthy relationship with data starts with a healthy relationship with yourself.

## The never-miss-twice rule

There's a popular rule among habit builders: you may miss once, but never twice in a row. Missing once is life — overtime, family events. Missing twice starts becoming a new pattern. This isn't a medical law, just a way of thinking that helps many people get back on track without drama.

## Celebrate the small stuff

Your brain needs proof that the effort matters. Eight weeks of streaks, one month of consistent logging, the first time you dared to look at the chart — all worth celebrating, even if the numbers haven't moved yet. Raga celebrates your milestones through the weekly Wrapped; you can celebrate them your own way too. Just one rule: don't celebrate by quitting.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'en') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('bmi-itu-apa', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'akademi-bmi' and locale = 'en'), 'What BMI Is (and Isn''t)', 'Body Mass Index: a simple formula that''s useful for screening — not your personal health report card.', $md$## The formula is simple

BMI (Body Mass Index) is your weight in kilograms divided by the square of your height in meters. One formula, no expensive equipment, calculable anywhere. That simplicity is exactly what makes it both popular and limited.

## The categories (for adults)

WHO uses these cutoffs for adults:

- Below 18.5: underweight
- 18.5–24.9: normal
- 25–29.9: overweight
- 30 and above: obesity

These cutoffs come from large population datasets and are used worldwide so data can be compared across countries.

## A brief history

The formula was born in the 1830s from Belgian mathematician Adolphe Quetelet. It was never designed to judge an individual's health — it was meant to study the average human body across populations. That fact matters: from day one, BMI has been a population statistics tool.

## What BMI is for

BMI is useful as a cheap, fast first screening step. For researchers and policymakers, it gives a big-picture view of population weight trends. For you, it's a starting point — a signal to pay attention, not a conclusion.

## What BMI is not for

BMI is not a diagnosis. It doesn't directly measure your body fat, doesn't know your muscle mass, and doesn't account for your age. Judging someone's health from BMI alone is like judging a book by its page count — there's information in it, but the story is incomplete.

## Why it's still used today

Despite all its limits, BMI survives for three reasons: it's cheap (no equipment needed), fast (one formula), and standard (data can be compared across regions and years). For large-scale initial screening, that combination is hard to beat. Problems arise when a screening tool gets used as the final verdict.

## BMI in Raga

In this app, BMI is the front door, not the whole house. Raga calculates it automatically, shows it on the gauge, then invites you to look further: trends over time, estimated calorie needs, and educational articles like the one you're reading. Think of BMI as the opening sentence of a conversation about your body — not the conclusion.$md$, 2, 'published', true, now(), now(), 'en') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('keterbatasan-bmi', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'akademi-bmi' and locale = 'en'), 'The Limits of BMI You Need to Know', 'BMI can''t tell muscle from fat — and that''s just one of its several limitations.', $md$## Muscle vs fat: its biggest problem

A kilogram of muscle and a kilogram of fat weigh the same on the scale, but BMI treats them identically. Athletes or regular strength trainers can land in the overweight category despite low body fat and good fitness. Conversely, a sedentary person can have a normal BMI with high body fat. BMI is blind to body composition.

## Age plays a role

As we age, muscle mass tends to decline while body fat can increase — at the same weight. So the same BMI number at 25 and 65 can tell very different stories. BMI also wasn't designed for pregnant women or growing children.

## Not all populations are the same

Research shows the link between BMI and health risk can differ across ethnic groups. Some Asian countries, for example, use slightly lower cutoffs because metabolic risk appears at smaller BMIs. The global benchmark is a useful starting point, but not a sacred number for everyone.

## Belly fat goes undetected

Two people with identical BMIs can store fat in different places — and fat around the belly (visceral) is linked to higher risk than fat on the hips or thighs. BMI sees none of this distribution.

## The takeaway

Use BMI alongside other indicators: waist circumference, fitness, blood pressure, blood sugar, and most importantly, a health professional's assessment. One number is never enough to conclude anything about someone's health — including yours.

## So what should you use instead?

The honest answer: a combination. BMI for a rough picture, waist circumference for fat distribution, and for a fuller picture, clinical checks like blood pressure, blood sugar, and lipid profiles. There is no single magic number that sums up your health — and that's actually good news: your health isn't determined by any single number.

## A healthier way to think

Instead of asking whether your BMI is normal, ask: do my habits support my body? Do I move enough, sleep enough, eat reasonably? Numbers can mislead, but habits rarely lie. Focus on what you can control today.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'en') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('bmi-anak-remaja', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'akademi-bmi' and locale = 'en'), 'BMI for Children and Teens: Don''t Use Adult Categories', 'Children''s bodies are still growing, so adult BMI categories don''t apply. What''s used instead is age-based BMI percentiles.', $md$## Why the categories differ

Adult BMI categories (18.5 / 25 / 30) were built from data on adults whose bodies have finished growing. Children and teens are still growing: their body composition changes fast with age and puberty stages. Applying adult cutoffs to a growing body isn't just inaccurate — it can mislead.

## What's actually used: age-based BMI percentiles

For children aged 2 and up, health professionals use age-and-sex-specific BMI growth charts. The result is read as a percentile: the 85th percentile, for example, means the child's BMI is higher than 85 percent of kids their age. This compares a child with peers their age, not with adults.

## Don't self-diagnose

Percentile charts are clinical tools. Reading them needs context: the child's growth history, family eating patterns, activity, and other factors. If you're worried about your child's weight, the right move is a conversation with a pediatrician or nutritionist — not judgment from a single number.

## For parents: watch your language

This matters. Children who get teased about weight — even as a joke — are at risk of body image issues and unhealthy eating patterns later in life. Keep the conversation focused on healthy family habits: eating together, moving together, sleeping enough. Not on the child's scale number.

## When to see a professional

Consult promptly if there's a drastic, unexplained weight change, or if the child shows signs of disordered eating. Early detection is always better, and treatment is the domain of professionals, not apps.

## What families can do

The home environment has huge influence. Keep fruit and water easy to reach, limit sugary drinks at home without drama, and get kids moving in fun ways — ball games, cycling, evening walks together. Enough sleep matters too, since poor sleep is linked to messy eating patterns. It's all about family habits, not monitoring a child's plate under a microscope.

## Focus on growing, not shrinking

The language adults use around children shapes how kids see their own bodies. Avoid commenting on anyone's body shape in front of children — including your own. A child who grows up with a healthy relationship to food and their body has something far more valuable than a perfect percentile number.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'en') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('otot-vs-lemak', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'akademi-bmi' and locale = 'en'), 'Muscle vs Fat: Why Two People With the Same BMI Can Be Worlds Apart', 'A kilo of muscle and a kilo of fat weigh the same — but they tell very different health stories.', $md$## Picture this

Imagine two people: both 75 kg and 175 cm, so their BMI is exactly the same, 24.5. One lifts weights three times a week; the other is completely sedentary. The scale numbers are twins, but one has a dense, muscular build and the other has high body fat. That's BMI's blind spot: it sees weight, not what's inside it.

## Why athletes can be "overweight"

Muscle is denser than fat — smaller in volume for the same weight. A muscular person can easily land in the BMI overweight category despite low body fat and great physical capacity. This isn't BMI being broken; it was never BMI's job to begin with.

## What matters more than the scale

If you train regularly, watch things other than the scale: a shrinking waistline, clothes fitting better, not getting winded climbing stairs, and training loads slowly going up. Those are all progress signals that one BMI number can't capture.

## Strength training for beginners

The good news: building muscle doesn't require a fancy gym. Two to three sessions a week with your own bodyweight — squats, push-ups, planks — is enough for beginners. Start light, keep good form, add gradually. Added muscle also helps your metabolism and everyday posture.

## A note of caution

This article isn't medical training advice. If you have specific health conditions — heart, joints, or you're pregnant — discuss your training plans with a professional before starting.

## Scale going up even though you train?

This is the classic beginner trap: you start strength training, eat more regularly, but the scale goes up a kilo or two. Don't panic yet. In the early phase, your body can add muscle while losing fat at the same time — total weight may stall or rise slightly while your body composition is actually improving. That's why the mirror, the tape measure, and better-fitting clothes are fairer judges than the scale alone.

## Be patient with the process

Building muscle is slow — think months, not weeks. But every training session is a deposit. As long as your waistline isn't growing and your strength is increasing, you're on the right track even if your BMI number isn't moving anywhere.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'en') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('lingkar-pinggang', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'akademi-bmi' and locale = 'en'), 'Waist Circumference: The Cheap and Easy Companion to BMI', 'Fat around the belly carries higher risk — and measuring it only takes a tape measure.', $md$## Why the belly gets the spotlight

Not all fat is created equal. Fat stored around the internal organs of the belly (visceral fat) is linked to higher metabolic risk than fat on the hips or thighs. BMI can't see where your fat is stored — that's where waist circumference helps.

## How to measure correctly

Stand straight and relaxed, wrap the tape around the midpoint between your lowest rib and the top of your hip bone. Keep the tape level, not pressing into the skin, and measure after a normal exhale — not while holding your breath or sucking in your stomach. Be honest; nobody's judging.

## The cutoffs

WHO uses these benchmarks for adult populations: risk starts rising at a waist circumference of 94 cm and above for men and 80 cm and above for women, with even higher risk at 102 cm and 88 cm. Remember, these are population screening benchmarks — your trend over time matters as much as the number itself.

## Not a replacement, a companion

Waist circumference doesn't replace BMI; it complements it. Together they paint a more honest picture: BMI speaks to weight relative to height, waist circumference speaks to fat distribution. Both are cheap, fast, and doable at home.

## When to consult someone

If your waist measurement is far above the cutoff and stays there, or keeps rising over months, use that as a reason to talk to a health professional — not to panic. The data you've collected actually makes that consultation far more productive.

## How often to measure

No need to do it daily — waist circumference changes far more slowly than body weight. Once a month is enough, under the same conditions (morning, before eating). Record it in the same place as your weight notes so it's easy to compare.

## Track it alongside BMI

The most practical combo for most people: BMI from Raga plus waist circumference once a month. If your BMI is dropping and your waistline is shrinking, that's a strong signal you're losing fat, not just water or muscle. Two simple numbers, a much fuller story.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'en') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('kalori-itu-energi', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'nutrisi-energi' and locale = 'en'), 'Calories Are Energy, Not the Enemy', 'Calories are treated like the villain. But a calorie is just a unit of energy — what matters is how you manage it consciously.', $md$## What is a calorie, really?

A calorie is just a unit of energy, like kilometers for distance or liters for volume. One kilocalorie (kcal — what food labels usually just call "calories") is the amount of energy your body needs to function. Your heart beating needs calories. Your brain thinking needs calories. Even lying on the couch watching a series burns calories.

So calories aren't poison, aren't the enemy, aren't something to fear. Without enough calories, your body can't function.

## Where do calories come from?

There are three main sources, which nutritionists call macronutrients:

- **Carbohydrates** — about 4 kcal per gram. Rice, bread, noodles, sweet potato, fruit.
- **Protein** — about 4 kcal per gram. Eggs, chicken, fish, tempeh, tofu.
- **Fat** — about 9 kcal per gram. Oil, coconut milk, nuts, avocado.

Fat is the most calorie-dense, which is why fried food feels heavy in calories even in small portions. But remember: calorie-dense doesn't mean evil. Your body still needs fat — for hormone production and vitamin absorption, for example.

## Why are calories treated like criminals?

Because popular diets built the narrative that calories equal the enemy. But what affects body weight isn't the existence of calories — it's the **overall pattern**: intake consistently exceeding needs over the long term, too little movement, too little sleep, and unmanaged stress.

Blaming a single number traps people at two extremes: fearing food until they're under-fueled, or giving up entirely. Neither is healthy.

## So what should you do?

You don't need to count every grain of rice, unless you enjoy it and it helps you. For most people, this is enough:

1. **Know your portions** — eat until comfortably full, not stuffed.
2. **Prioritize whole foods** — things still recognizable as food, not ultra-processed products.
3. **Watch your drinks** — liquid calories from sweet drinks and sugary milk coffee often go unnoticed but add up fast.
4. **Consistent, not perfect** — one day of overeating ruins nothing. What matters is the pattern over weeks.

Calories are information, not a verdict. Use the information to make more conscious decisions, not to punish yourself. If you have a medical condition related to eating patterns, discuss your intake targets with a doctor or dietitian.$md$, 2, 'published', true, now(), now(), 'en') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('bmr-tdee-bahasa-manusia', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'nutrisi-energi' and locale = 'en'), 'BMR and TDEE, Explained Like a Human', 'These two abbreviations show up everywhere in calorie talk. Here''s the simple version: what they mean, and how to use them without becoming a slave to numbers.', $md$## BMR: your body's cost of living

BMR (Basal Metabolic Rate) is the estimated energy your body burns in a day **if you just lay in bed for 24 hours** — for breathing, heartbeat, brain work, and maintaining body temperature. The number is influenced by weight, height, age, and sex.

Popular formulas like Mifflin-St Jeor are used by many apps, including Raga, to estimate BMR. Worth emphasizing: this is an **estimate from a general formula**, not a lab measurement of your body.

## TDEE: your total daily burn

TDEE (Total Daily Energy Expenditure) is your BMR plus all your activity: walking to the store, climbing stairs, exercising, even the habit of bouncing your leg. That's why TDEE is calculated by multiplying BMR by an activity factor, from barely moving to very active.

This number is used as the benchmark for your daily calorie needs.

## Why can two people differ so much?

Because their body composition and activity differ. People with more muscle mass usually have a higher BMR, since muscle is expensive for the body to maintain. Someone doing physical work all day can have double the TDEE of an office worker with a similar BMR.

So don't compare your calorie needs with someone else's. Compare them with **your own body's trend** over time.

## How to use these numbers sanely

1. **Treat it as a starting point, not scripture.** Start from the TDEE estimate, then watch your weight trend for 2 to 4 weeks.
2. **Change slowly.** If your goal is weight loss, a small deficit — say 300 to 500 kcal below TDEE — is far more sustainable than a brutal cut.
3. **Don't eat far below your BMR continuously** without professional supervision. That's a recipe for a sluggish body and a metabolism adapting in ways you don't want.
4. **Recalculate periodically.** Weight changes, BMR follows. Update your estimate every few months.

Formulas are tools, not diagnoses. If you have a medical condition like a thyroid disorder or diabetes, discuss your calorie targets with a doctor or dietitian instead of relying on app numbers alone.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'en') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('protein-fondasi', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'nutrisi-energi' and locale = 'en'), 'Protein: The Foundation You''re Probably Missing', 'Not just for people who want muscles. Protein is the body''s building material and the most satiating macronutrient — and many people don''t get enough.', $md$## What is protein actually for?

Many think protein is only for people who want to build muscle. But protein is the building material for almost everything in your body: muscles, skin, hair, digestive enzymes, hormones, even the immune system. Long-term protein deficiency slows recovery, makes you get sick easily, and shrinks muscle mass — especially while losing weight.

The good news: protein is also the **most satiating** macronutrient. Protein-rich food keeps you full longer than the same calories from refined carbs.

## How much do you need?

A commonly used general benchmark is about **0.8 grams per kilogram of body weight per day** for adults with light activity. People who exercise regularly usually need more, but the exact number is better discussed with an expert.

This is a general population guideline, not a personal prescription. Pregnant and breastfeeding women, older adults, and people with kidney disease have different needs. For special conditions, consult a doctor or dietitian.

## Easy-to-find protein sources

No need for plain boiled chicken breast every day:

- **Animal-based:** eggs, chicken, affordable fish like mackerel and catfish, beef, milk and yogurt.
- **Plant-based:** tempeh, tofu, edamame, beans. Tempeh is a local champion: cheap, protein-dense, and its fermentation is good for digestion.

Combine both so you don't get bored and your wallet stays safe.

## Worried about "incomplete" plant protein?

Animal protein generally contains all essential amino acids in good proportions, while most plant proteins lack one or two types. The good news: you don't need to complete them in a single plate. As long as you eat varied foods throughout the day — say tempeh at lunch and eggs at night — your body gets everything it needs. So even a plant-based diet can meet protein needs with simple planning, without excessive stress.

## Simple ways to add protein

- Add **one protein source to every main meal**. Been eating just rice and veggies? Add an egg or tempeh.
- A protein-rich breakfast, like eggs or yogurt, drastically reduces snack cravings by midday for many people.
- Swap snacks: boiled peanuts or edamame instead of chips.

You don't need expensive supplement powders to start. Everyday food in the right portions already makes a big difference.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'en') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('serat-kenyang-lebih-lama', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'nutrisi-energi' and locale = 'en'), 'Fiber: The Easiest Way to Stay Full Longer', 'Fiber isn''t digested by the body — and that''s exactly its advantage: it adds food volume, slows hunger, and keeps digestion healthy.', $md$## What is fiber?

Fiber is the part of plant food that your body **doesn't digest**. Sounds useless, but that's exactly its advantage: fiber adds volume to food without meaningful calories, slows digestion, and feeds the good bacteria in your gut.

## Why does fiber keep you full longer?

Three simple mechanisms:

1. **Volume** — fibrous food takes up space in your stomach, so fullness signals arrive faster.
2. **Slow digestion** — blood sugar rises gently instead of spiking and crashing like after a sweet drink, which usually leaves you hungry again an hour later.
3. **Needs chewing** — eating becomes slower, and your brain gets time to realize you're full.

That's why a bowl of veggies plus protein keeps you full for hours, while the same calories from a sweet drink vanish without a trace.

## Everyday fiber sources

- Vegetables: spinach, water spinach, broccoli, carrots. Add a handful every meal.
- Whole fruit, not juice: papaya, banana, apple, orange.
- Beans and legumes: kidney beans, mung beans.
- Whole carbs: brown rice, oatmeal, corn, sweet potato.

A commonly cited general benchmark is about **25 to 30 grams per day** for adults. In reality, most people are still far below that number.

## Soluble vs insoluble fiber — do you need to know?

Soluble fiber, found in oatmeal, apples, and beans, dissolves in water to form a gel that slows digestion and helps control blood sugar and cholesterol. Insoluble fiber, found in leafy greens, fruit skins, and whole grains, adds bulk to stool and keeps bowel movements regular. You don't need to memorize the list. Just eat a variety from both groups every day, and your body gets the benefits of both.

## Increase gradually, and don't forget water

Don't jump from zero straight to 30 grams — your stomach will protest with bloating. Increase gradually over one to two weeks, and **drink enough water**, because fiber needs water to work properly. Fiber without enough water can actually cause constipation.

The practical rule is simple: half your plate should be vegetables and fruit at every main meal. Cheap, easy, and the effects are felt within days.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'en') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('diet-ekstrem-vs-pola-makan', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'nutrisi-energi' and locale = 'en'), 'Extreme Diets vs. Eating Patterns That Actually Last', 'Extreme diets promise fast results, but the evidence says they backfire: muscle loss, yo-yo weight, and a broken relationship with food. Here''s the sensible alternative.', $md$## Why are extreme diets so tempting?

The promise of losing many kilograms in two weeks is addictive. Extreme diets — total fasting for days, eating only one type of food, or super-low calories without supervision — offer a shortcut. And shortcuts always sell.

The problem: what drops fast in the first weeks is mostly **water and muscle mass**, not fat. Once the diet stops, the weight comes back, often higher than before. This is called the yo-yo effect, and the pattern is well documented in health literature.

## Why are extreme diets dangerous?

This part matters, so read slowly:

- **You lose muscle, not just fat.** An extreme deficit forces your body to break down muscle for energy. Muscle is hard to build and easy to lose.
- **Your body adapts.** Your energy expenditure can drop in response to prolonged starvation. That's why weight stalls, then surges again when you eat normally.
- **Nutrient deficiency.** Wiping out whole food groups without replacements means vitamin and mineral deficiencies.
- **A broken relationship with food.** Rigid rules, guilt over every small slip, cycles of restriction followed by bingeing. If you feel you've lost control over your eating patterns, **seek professional help**. That's not weakness — it's a brave step.
- **Serious medical risks** from very-low-calorie diets without supervision, including heart rhythm disturbances, gallstones, and electrolyte imbalances.

## Warning signs of a diet to be suspicious of

- Promises fast, guaranteed results.
- Bans entire food groups without medical reason.
- Requires buying specific products or supplements.
- Has rigid rules: one small slip counts as total failure.
- Never suggests consulting a health professional.

## The alternative: a balanced eating pattern

Not a 30-day program, but an **eating pattern**: how you eat day to day, year after year.

1. **Small deficit, patiently.** Losing about 0.5 kg per week is a healthy, sustainable pace for most people.
2. **No food is totally banned.** What exists is portion and frequency.
3. **Enough protein and fiber** to stay full and preserve muscle mass.
4. **Flexible.** Family event or holiday? Enjoy it, then return to your normal pattern. One day ruins nothing.
5. **Guided by a professional** if you have a medical condition.

Your body isn't a 30-day project. Build a way of eating you'll still be doing five years from now. That's what actually works.$md$, 3, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'en') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('tidur-dan-berat-badan', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'kebiasaan-sehat' and locale = 'en'), 'Sleep and Body Weight: The Underrated Link', 'Poor sleep disrupts hunger hormones and pushes you toward high-calorie food. Fixing your sleep might be the most underrated step in your journey.', $md$## Staying up late makes you hungry all next day

This isn't just a feeling — there's a mechanism. Sleep deprivation disrupts two appetite-regulating hormones: **ghrelin** the hunger trigger goes up, while **leptin** the fullness signal goes down. The result: you feel hungry even though your body doesn't need extra energy.

Research also shows sleep-deprived people tend to choose higher-calorie foods in larger portions. A tired brain struggles to say enough.

## Food decisions get worse when you're tired

Think back: when was the last time you stayed up late and ended up ordering fried snacks at 11 PM? When tired, the part of your brain that thinks long-term weakens, while the reward center becomes more reactive to sweet and fatty foods. This isn't about weak willpower — the biology really works that way.

## How many hours is enough?

The general recommendation for adults is **7 to 9 hours per night**. This is a population guideline, not a personal verdict, since everyone's needs differ slightly. Teenagers need more, around 8 to 10 hours. If you routinely sleep under 6 hours and feel sleepy all day, that's a clear signal to change.

## Sleep is also about recovery

During deep sleep, your body repairs muscle, regulates hormones, and consolidates memory — including the memory of the good habits you built during the day. Exercise without enough sleep means half-baked recovery.

## How to sleep better without buying expensive gadgets

- **Consistent schedule.** Wake up at the same time every day, including weekends. This is the most powerful strategy.
- **Dark, cool, quiet bedroom.** Your body needs clear signals that it's rest time.
- **Less screen time 30 to 60 minutes before bed**, or at least avoid content that stirs emotions.
- **Limit caffeine** after midday. Its effects can last 6 to 8 hours.
- **Avoid very intense exercise close to bedtime** if it keeps you from falling asleep.

One important note: if you snore loudly, seem to stop breathing during sleep, or feel heavily drowsy during the day all the time, it could be a sign of sleep apnea or another sleep disorder. **See a doctor** and don't self-diagnose.$md$, 2, 'published', true, now(), now(), 'en') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('jalan-kaki-underrated', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'kebiasaan-sehat' and locale = 'en'), 'Walking: The Most Underrated Exercise', 'Zero barriers, doable by anyone, with real effects on heart, weight, and stress. Here''s how to start sanely.', $md$## Why is walking so powerful?

Because the barrier is zero: no gym, no special skills, no specific outfit needed. But the effects are real: regular walking improves heart health, helps weight management, stabilizes blood sugar, and — often forgotten — **reduces stress**.

Walking also counts as NEAT, short for Non-Exercise Activity Thermogenesis: energy burned from daily activities outside formal exercise. For most people, NEAT is actually the biggest contributor to differences in daily energy expenditure. Meaning, someone who walks a lot in daily life can burn far more than someone who exercises 30 minutes then sits for ten hours.

## It doesn't have to be 10,000 steps right away

The 10,000 number started as a pedometer marketing slogan, not a medical decree. What matters is **starting from where you are now, then increasing gradually**.

- Under 3,000 steps a day? Aim for 5,000 first.
- Already at 5,000? Move up to 7,000–8,000.
- Adding 500 to 1,000 steps per week is good progress.

Being consistent at 7,000 steps every day beats 15,000 steps that only happens twice a month.

## Ways to sneak walking into your day

- Get off public transport one stop earlier.
- Take phone calls while walking, not sitting.
- Park in the farthest spot. Free and healthy.
- Walk 10 minutes after meals. Bonus: it helps blood sugar too.
- Bring a friend or listen to a podcast so you don't get bored.

## Start this week

No complicated plan needed. Pick one trigger that already exists: a ten-minute walk after lunch, or fifteen minutes after work. Do it five days this week, then log it in Raga or your calendar. Next week add five minutes. Small recorded progress motivates far more than a big target that never gets started.

## Walking vs running — which wins?

Both are good. Running burns more per minute, but it's harder on the joints and more people quit by week two. Walking can be done by almost anyone, almost every day, for years.

The best exercise isn't the coolest one — it's the one **you're still doing six months from now**. If you have joint, heart, or other medical conditions, discuss your exercise options with a doctor first.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'en') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('konsistensi-vs-intensitas', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'kebiasaan-sehat' and locale = 'en'), 'Consistency Beats Intensity', 'Three days of fire then total surrender is the classic pattern. Small habits that survive a year beat heroic programs that last a week.', $md$## The three-day-motivation trap

A familiar pattern: Monday starts a strict diet plus two hours of exercise. Wednesday still going. Friday already ordering fried snacks. Sunday gives up entirely, waiting for next Monday to restart.

The problem isn't your intention — it's the **strategy**. Giant changes need giant energy, and that energy runs out fast. What's usually left is just guilt.

## The math of small habits

A one-percent difference each day sounds trivial, but behavior compounds like savings:

- Walking 20 minutes daily for a year equals **more than 120 hours** of movement.
- Cutting one sweet drink a day means **tens of thousands of kcal** in a year that never went in.
- Weighing once a week means **52 data points** for reading your trend.

None of these is heroic. All are sustainable. That's the point.

## How to make habits stick

1. **Small until it feels silly.** The 30-minute workout target always fails? Lower it to putting on your sports shoes and walking five minutes. The bar for starting must be as low as possible.
2. **Specific.** Not "eat healthier," but "add vegetables to lunch every workday."
3. **Attach to an old routine.** Example: after brushing your teeth at night, prepare tomorrow's water bottle. New habits stick easiest to old ones.
4. **Log it.** Checked on a calendar or logged in Raga. Visible progress makes your brain crave continuing.

## Environment beats willpower

Willpower is a resource that gets used up, while environment works without you noticing. Put your sports shoes by the door, store sweet snacks somewhere hard to reach, prepare tomorrow's lunch the night before. Consistent people usually aren't the ones with the strongest willpower — they're the ones smartest at designing their days so the good choice becomes the easiest choice.

## One missed day isn't failure

Habit research shows what makes people succeed isn't perfection, but **how fast they get back**. Missed a day? Continue tomorrow like nothing happened. What's dangerous isn't the miss — it's the "ah whatever, total failure" story that turns one missed day into a missed month.

The target isn't a hundred perfect days. The target is simple: **never miss twice in a row**.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'en') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('cara-menimbang-yang-benar', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'kebiasaan-sehat' and locale = 'en'), 'How to Weigh Yourself So the Data Is Honest', 'Many people weigh carelessly, then panic or celebrate over numbers that are actually just noise. Here''s how to get comparable data.', $md$## A scale is only as good as how you use it

Many people weigh carelessly: different times, different clothes, right after a big meal — then panic or celebrate over numbers that are actually just noise. What you need isn't a perfect number, but **numbers you can compare** over time.

## The ground rules

1. **Same time.** Morning after waking up and using the bathroom, before eating or drinking. This is the most consistent condition.
2. **Same conditions.** Minimal or the same clothes every time. Don't compare a t-shirt morning with a jeans night.
3. **Same scale and surface.** Hard, flat floor. Switching scales means switching numbers.
4. **A frequency you can handle.** Once a week is enough for most people. Daily is fine if you're the type who doesn't get emotional over fluctuations. If daily numbers stress you out, go weekly.

## Don't panic over daily fluctuations

Body weight can swing **0.5 to 2 kg in a single day**. The causes are all normal: drinking water, undigested food, salt retaining water, lack of sleep, stress, menstrual cycle, and for exercisers, muscle inflammation after training.

Gaining one kilogram tomorrow morning doesn't mean you gained one kilogram of fat. It takes a surplus of about 7,700 kcal to gain one kilogram of fat. Almost impossible overnight.

## What matters: the trend, not the dot

Look at weekly averages or two-to-four-week trends. Daily ups and downs are weather; the trend is climate. Raga draws trend charts precisely for this: so you read the climate instead of panicking over the weather.

## When the scale isn't the right tool

- **Pregnancy.** Weight gain is normal and necessary. Targets are discussed with a doctor or midwife.
- **History of eating disorders.** Numbers can be a trigger. Focus on habits with professional guidance.
- **Certain medical conditions** like heart failure or kidney disorders, where weight fluctuations can be a medical signal. Follow your doctor's guidance.

A scale is a measuring tool, not a judge. Used right, it's a friend. Used wrong, it's a free source of stress.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'en') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();
insert into public.knowledge_articles (slug, category_id, title, summary, content_md, reading_minutes, status, featured, published_at, reviewed_at, locale) values ('mindful-eating', (select id from public.knowledge_categories where slug = 'kebiasaan-sehat' and locale = 'en'), 'Mindful Eating Without the Drama', 'Not a diet and not rigid rules. Mindful eating is just about being present while eating — and that''s enough to change a lot.', $md$## What is mindful eating?

Mindful eating means eating with full attention. Not a diet, not allowed-versus-forbidden rules, not a complicated ritual. Simply: **be present while eating**. Feel the taste, texture, and your body's fullness signals.

Sounds trivial until you realize how often we eat while scrolling our phones, watching, or working — then suddenly the plate is empty and we don't remember the taste.

## Why does eating while scrolling lead to overeating?

Your brain needs about **20 minutes** to receive fullness signals from your stomach. If your attention is on a screen, you eat faster and those signals get missed. The result: the same portion feels insufficient, even though your body actually had enough.

This isn't about discipline — it's about attention design. Change the design, and the behavior follows.

## Four simple steps to start

1. **Put away screens** while eating, starting with just one meal a day. It doesn't have to be all of them at once.
2. **Eat slower.** Put your spoon down between bites. Chew properly. Sounds silly, the effect is real.
3. **Use a 1-to-10 fullness scale.** Before eating ask yourself: how hungry am I? Mid-meal: where am I now? Stop around 7 to 8 — comfortably full, not a stuffed 10.
4. **Tell physical hunger apart from emotional hunger.** Physical hunger comes gradually and accepts many kinds of food. Emotional hunger arrives suddenly and craves one specific thing, usually sweet or savory. If it's the latter, try a ten-minute pause and a glass of water first.

## It's not about being perfect

Mindful eating doesn't mean every bite must become meditation. Eating while chatting with family is also mindful, because your attention is in the moment. The target isn't ritual perfection — it's **being present more often** than on autopilot.

One important boundary: if your relationship with food has reached a disruptive point — for example uncontrollable overeating, extreme guilt, or other dangerous patterns — that's beyond this article's territory. **Seek professional help.** No internet tips can replace it.$md$, 2, 'published', false, now(), now(), 'en') on conflict (slug, locale) do update set title = excluded.title, summary = excluded.summary, content_md = excluded.content_md, reading_minutes = excluded.reading_minutes, status = 'published', featured = excluded.featured, reviewed_at = now(), updated_at = now();

